course: language
lesson: 53
title: Told you a hundred times: how other languages exaggerate and play down
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every language exaggerates and plays things down, but each one picks its own pictures. An English mum has told you «a million times», a Spanish mum «a thousand times» (mil veces), a Ukrainian mum «a hundred times» (сто разів, sto raziv). Different numbers, the same meaning: «lots, and I'm cross».
explain: Picture Mei's family in Beijing: her aunt flops on the sofa and says «lèi sǐ le», «tired to death»; her cousin smells dumplings, «è sǐ le», «hungry to death»; her sister giggles at a cartoon, «xiào sǐ wǒ le», «laughing me to death». Nobody is dying: in Mandarin, «sǐ le» (dead) just means «very».
explain: Other languages use animals and things: a French girl has «a wolf's hunger», a French morning is «duck cold», and an Italian nonna loves you «a sackful».
explain: Playing down travels too: a Ukrainian grandma says «Непогано!» (nepohano, «not bad») about a very good mark, and a Japanese neighbour calls her lovely present «just a boring little thing» to be polite.
explain: So when you hear a strange picture in another language, don't translate it word for word: ask what feeling it carries.
fact: «Я тобі сто разів казала!» (Ya tobi sto raziv kazala) is Ukrainian for «I told you a hundred times!»; it means many times, and the speaker is cross.
fact: «¡Te lo he dicho mil veces!» is Spanish for «I've told you a thousand times!»; English uses a million, Spanish a thousand, Ukrainian a hundred, and all mean «lots».
fact: In Mandarin Chinese, «sǐ le» (dead) after a word makes it stronger: «lèi sǐ le» means very tired and «è sǐ le» means very hungry, like English «dead tired» or «starving».
fact: «xiào sǐ wǒ le» («laughing me to death») means «it's really funny», just like English «I'm dead 💀»; many languages use «death» to mean «very much».
fact: «Чекаю цілу вічність» (Chekayu tsilu vichnist) is Ukrainian for «I've been waiting a whole eternity», the same as English «forever».
fact: «Непогано!» (Nepohano) means «not bad» in Ukrainian and, like British «not bad», it often means «good»; litotes exists in Ukrainian too.
fact: «J'ai une faim de loup!» («I've got a wolf's hunger») is French for «I'm very hungry», where English says «I could eat a horse».
fact: «Il fait un froid de canard!» («It's duck cold!») is an old French saying for «it's very cold».
fact: In Japan, «tsumaranai mono desu ga» («it's just a boring little thing, but…») is a traditional polite phrase when giving a gift; the gift is probably lovely.
fact: In Italian, «Ti voglio un sacco di bene» means «I love you very much»; «un sacco» (a sack) means «loads», like English «a ton».
fact: Fixed sayings can't be translated word for word: you translate the meaning («very hungry»), not the picture (a wolf).
exercise: Taras's mum in Kyiv: «Я тобі сто разів казала!» («I told you a hundred times!»). What does she mean? | Many times, and she's cross | Exactly one hundred times | Only once or twice | She's really happy with Taras today
exercise: Pablo's mum in Madrid says «¡Te lo he dicho mil veces!». How many times has she told him? | Many times | A thousand times exactly | Never at all | Just this once
exercise: English says «a million times». Which number does Spanish use in this saying? | A thousand | A hundred | A million | A hundred thousand
exercise: Mei's aunt flops on the sofa after work: «lèi sǐ le» («tired to death»). What does she mean? | She's very tired | Someone has died | She's very ill | She's very cross
exercise: In Mandarin, what does «sǐ le» (dead) do after a word like «tired» or «hungry»? | It makes the word stronger, like «very» | It means somebody has really died | It makes the word into a question | It turns the word into its exact opposite meaning
exercise: Mei's cousin smells dumplings: «è sǐ le!». What does he mean? | He's very hungry | He's in danger | He hates dumplings | He's very full
exercise: Mei's sister watches a cartoon: «xiào sǐ wǒ le!». What is the English that's closest? | «I'm dead 💀», it's hilarious | «I'm scared, please turn it off now» | «This is so sad» | «I'm bored of this»
exercise: Olena's grandad in Lviv: «Чекаю цілу вічність» («I've been waiting a whole eternity»). How long has he probably waited? | Maybe twenty minutes | A whole eternity | Since the day he was born | About a year
exercise: Grandma sees Olena's very good mark: «Непогано!» («not bad»). How does she feel? | Pleased | Disappointed | Worried | Bored
exercise: Léa comes home from swimming: «J'ai une faim de loup!». What does she mean? | She's very hungry | She's like a wolf | She saw a wolf | She's very tired
exercise: A cold morning in Lyon: «Il fait un froid de canard!». What does it mean? | It's very cold | It's good weather for ducks | There are ducks outside | It's raining a little
exercise: Yuki's mum gives a beautiful present: «tsumaranai mono desu ga» («it's just a boring little thing, but…»). Why does she say that? | It's a polite custom when giving a gift | She thinks the present is really boring | She doesn't like the neighbour | She bought it in a cheap shop
exercise: Marco's nonna says «Ti voglio un sacco di bene». What does «un sacco» mean here? | Loads | A sack | A bag of shopping | A little
exercise: Which English saying matches French «a wolf's hunger»? | «I could eat a horse» | «I'm a little bit peckish today» | «It's not bad» | «I'm dead tired»
exercise: How should you translate a saying like «a wolf's hunger» into English? | By its meaning: «very hungry» | Word for word: «wolf's hunger» | By leaving it out of the text | By drawing a picture of a wolf
test: «so Ukrainian mums only say a hundred times, English mums say a million, so English mums are way crosser lol» What is right? | No, the numbers are different but the meaning is the same: lots, and cross | Yes, a million is bigger, so English mums are much crosser | Yes, Spanish mums are in the middle at a thousand | No, Ukrainian mums are much crosser, because a hundred is a real, countable number
test: Your friend hears Mei's aunt say «lèi sǐ le» and gasps «who died??». What do you tell her? | Nobody; it means she's very tired, like «dead tired» | Her aunt's old friend from work has sadly died this week | It is a sad song in Chinese | Her aunt is going to bed for ever
test: ★ «xiào sǐ wǒ le» and English «I'm dead 💀» are both about laughing. What do they show about many languages? | They use «death» to mean «very much» | They never joke about laughing | They all copied it from English speakers online | They only use it for sad things
test: ★ A tourist hears «Il fait un froid de canard!» and goes looking for cold ducks. What has he done? | Translated the picture instead of the meaning | Understood the saying exactly right | Found the right place for duck spotting | Confused a duck with a goose
test: ★ Olena cries because Grandma only said «Непогано!» about her very good mark. What should Olena know? | «Not bad» in Ukrainian, as in British English, often means «good» | Grandma thinks the mark was only just okay | Grandma wanted a mark that was much higher | «Непогано» is a word Grandma uses when she is cross
test: Lucy's neighbour in Japan gives her a beautiful scarf: «it's just a boring little thing». Lucy says «oh, okay» and puts it away. What would have been better? | A big thank you, because playing it down is a polite custom | Agreeing with her that it is boring | Giving it back, since she didn't like it | Asking how much it cost in the shop
test: «Marco's nonna loves him a sackful?? that's so weird» What is the best answer? | It's Italian for «loads», like English «a ton» | It means she keeps him in a sack | It means she loves him only a little | It's a rude word in Italian
test: ★★ Léa's «wolf's hunger», Grandad's «I could eat a horse», and Mei's cousin's «hungry to death». What do all three mean? | Very hungry | A bit peckish | Scared of animals | Feeling ill
test: ★★★ English «a million times», Spanish «mil veces» (a thousand), Ukrainian «сто разів» (a hundred). A mum told you off 12 real times. Which saying gives the real number? | None of them | The Ukrainian one | The Spanish one | The English one
test: ★★★ Which pair is one hyperbole and one understatement? | French «a wolf's hunger» and Ukrainian «Непогано» | Spanish «mil veces» and English «a million times» | Mandarin «è sǐ le» and French «a wolf's hunger» | Japanese «a boring little thing» and Ukrainian «Непогано»
exam: Identify the meaning of the Mandarin expression «lèi sǐ le», uttered by a person who has returned from work. | That the speaker is exceedingly tired | That a death has occurred | That the speaker is unwell | That the speaker is angry
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Spanish «mil veces», the Ukrainian «сто разів» and the English «a million times»? | They employ different numbers to express one meaning: many times | They express three different quantities which may be compared | Only the Ukrainian expression is a measurement | Only the English expression is an exaggeration
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a giver in Japan may describe a costly gift as «a boring little thing». | It is a customary expression of politeness | It is an accurate assessment of the gift | It is an expression of dislike for the recipient | It is a request for the return of the gift
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principles of translation in respect of the French «J'ai une faim de loup». | It is to be rendered by its meaning, «I am very hungry» | It is to be rendered word for word as «I have a wolf's hunger» and no more | It is to be omitted as untranslatable | It is to be rendered as «I have seen a wolf»
